<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Winter at War</h1><p class="path-tagline">Campaigns and sieges that ice, snow, cold and hunger decided or shaped.</p><p class="path-intro">Winter has served some armies and destroyed others. In 1242 Alexander Nevsky defeated the Livonian Order on frozen Lake Peipus, and in 1658 a Swedish king marched across the frozen Belts to force a treaty on Denmark. These stops then follow the frosts of 1708–1709 that killed thousands in the Swedish army invading Russia, Washington's crossing of the icy Delaware and the winter camp at Valley Forge, and the retreat of 1812 that destroyed most of Napoleon's army. The last stops are the Finnish ski troops of the Winter War and the siege of Leningrad, where many residents starved in the winter of 1941–1942.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-ice"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-ice.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1242</span><span class="path-step-name">Battle on the Ice</span><span class="path-step-why">On 5 April 1242 Alexander Nevsky's Novgorodians defeated the Livonian Order on frozen Lake Peipus, which checked the crusaders' expansion into Russian lands.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Battle on the Ice was fought on 5 April 1242 on frozen Lake Peipus between the forces of the Novgorod Republic under Prince Alexander Nevsky and an army of the Livonian Order and the Bishopric of Dorpat.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/swedish-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 1611 CE – 1721 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Swedish Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">In 1658 Charles X Gustav marched across the frozen Belts and forced the Treaty of Roskilde on Denmark; in 1709 defeat at Poltava broke Swedish power.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Swedish Empire refers to the period, known in Swedish as Stormaktstiden or the Age of Great Power, when Sweden dominated the Baltic region in the seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/great-frost-of-1709"><span class="path-step-ph">3</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1708–1709</span><span class="path-step-name">Great Frost of 1709</span><span class="path-step-why">In Europe's coldest winter of the past 500 years, storms and frosts killed thousands in the Swedish army invading Russia, contributing to its defeat at Poltava.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Great Frost, as it was known in England, or Le Grand Hiver in France, was an extraordinarily cold winter in Europe in 1708–1709, the coldest European winter of the past 500 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/george-washingtons-crossing-of-the-delaware-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-washingtons-crossing-of-the-delaware-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1776</span><span class="path-step-name">George Washington's crossing of the Delaware River</span><span class="path-step-why">On the night of 25–26 December 1776 Washington led a column across the icy Delaware in rain, sleet and snow to surprise the Hessians at Trenton.</span><span class="path-step-lede">George Washington's crossing of the Delaware River, on the night of 25–26 December 1776, was the first move in a surprise attack by the Continental Army on the Hessian forces garrisoned at Trenton during the American Revolutionary War.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/valley-forge"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valley-forge.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1777–1778</span><span class="path-step-name">Valley Forge</span><span class="path-step-why">In winter quarters from December 1777 an estimated 1,700 to 2,000 soldiers died of disease, possibly made worse by malnutrition and cold, wet weather.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Valley Forge was the winter encampment of the Continental Army under George Washington in eastern Pennsylvania, along the Schuylkill River, during the Philadelphia campaign of the American Revolutionary War.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/russian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 1721 CE – 1917 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Russian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The empire proclaimed in 1721 after the defeat of Sweden was invaded in 1812 by Napoleon's Grande Armée, which was destroyed in the winter retreat.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Russian Empire was the state ruled by the Romanov dynasty from Peter the Great's proclamation of the empire in 1721 until the revolution of 1917.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/french-invasion-of-russia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-invasion-of-russia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1812</span><span class="path-step-name">French invasion of Russia</span><span class="path-step-why">The Russian army retreated and fought at Borodino, Moscow burned after its occupation, and Napoleon's retreat in winter destroyed most of his army.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The French invasion of Russia was Napoleon's campaign of 1812, known in Russia as the Patriotic War.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/republic-of-finland"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elias-lonnrot.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1917 CE – 2025 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Republic of Finland</span><span class="path-step-why">Independent since 1917, Finland was attacked by the Soviet Union in 1939 and fought the Winter War, losing Karelia but keeping its independence.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-suomussalmi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-suomussalmi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1939–1940</span><span class="path-step-name">Battle of Suomussalmi</span><span class="path-step-why">From December 1939 Finnish ski troops cut two road-bound Soviet divisions into isolated pockets in the forests of north-eastern Finland and destroyed them.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Battle of Suomussalmi was fought in the forests of north-eastern Finland from December 1939 to January 1940, during the Winter War.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-leningrad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-leningrad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1941–1944</span><span class="path-step-name">Siege of Leningrad</span><span class="path-step-why">The Germans decided to starve the city: many residents starved in the winter of 1941–1942, and supplies crossed Lake Ladoga in winter over the Road of Life on the ice.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The siege of Leningrad was a military blockade of the Soviet city of Leningrad, present-day Saint Petersburg, by Germany and Finland on the Eastern Front of World War II; it lasted 872 days, and the city was never captured.</span></span></a></li></ol>
